00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.420
SPEAKER: With almost 500
sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.420
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.240
So if you didn't see Serverless
on Google Cloud, What's New,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.760
then stay tuned,
because here's a rewind.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.420
Serverless technology
continues to innovate

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.330
in the cloud namespace, where
that platform takes away

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.330
all the complexity that comes
with handling the lower level

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.940
infrastructure so you get
to focus on what matters,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:32.960
your code.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.770
In this talk, we'll discuss all
the new and exciting changes

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.460
since last year.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.560
First, we start
with an announcement

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.860
that GCP has updated the base
image for serverless offerings

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.980
to Ubuntu 18.4,
which gives access

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.380
to a broader set of
system libraries,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.350
and also enables some new
capabilities for the product I

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.860
write documentation for, App
Engine, who is celebrating

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.750
its 10th birthday this year.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.170
Originally shipped in 2008
with support for only Python,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.770
this year, App Engine rolls out
its second generation runtimes,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:08.900
giving you access to Node.js
8, Python 3.7, and PHP 7.2.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.780
And while App Engine is
celebrating its longevity,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.540
Google Cloud Functions is
celebrating its first year

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.120
with a whole set
of announcements.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.570
First, Cloud Functions is now
available for all developers,

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:24.500
making it production ready
and even provides an SLA.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.290
Along with that is
support for more regions,

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.450
scaling controls so you
can put an upper limit

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.490
on the number of
instances of a function,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.500
and languages, including Node
11 and Python 3.7, both of which

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.680
contain common features
like logs, exceptions,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.770
error reporting, and alerts,
all through Stackdriver.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.100
Another great new GCF feature
is environment variables,

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.580
specific key value pairs that
are bound to a single function

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.950
but don't exist inside
your source code

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.600
you set them at deploy
time and they'll

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.500
be injected as literal
environment variables

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.860
at execution time.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.080
Continuing with the
updates, GCF now

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.900
has CloudSQL direct
connection support, as well as

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:07.190
access and IAM support through
VPC interconnect to a network.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.440
Control access to
function invocation

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.480
using IAM on the invocation.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.330
You can assign the role of
invoker to a particular user,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.410
allowing you to define who
can kick off which functions.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.040
Great for a function
to function use cases.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.770
Now, something I'm
really excited about

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.120
is the availability of a
new tool, Cloud Scheduling,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.400
which allows you to schedule
HTTP Pub/Sub tasks down

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.960
to one minute intervals
in both GCF over HTTPS

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.110
and invoke App Engine
on relative URL.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.150
You can start setting
up timers for events we

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.550
want to happen, like Cron jobs.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.900
For more information
on all these updates,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.390
including a few live
demos of the technology

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.600
and a top secret
product release,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.940
check out the full video link
in the description below.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.570
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Google Cloud Platform

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.990
YouTube channel for more
great Next Rewind content.

